## Environment

* MSVC 19.29.30159 (Visual Studio 2019 16.11), x64, `/permissive-`. Not tested with Visual Studio 2022.
* gz-physics main. gz-physics9 has the same `detail/RequestFeatures.hh`.

## Description

Files that include `gz/physics/RequestFeatures.hh` get warning C4103 at their next `#include`, although gz-physics has no `#pragma pack`. In gz-sim:

```
src\systems\physics\Physics.hh(27): warning C4103: alignment changed after including header, may be due to missing #pragma pack(pop)
src\systems\physics\Physics.cc(18): warning C4103: alignment changed after including header, may be due to missing #pragma pack(pop)
src\systems\physics\EntityFeatureMap_TEST.cc(20): warning C4103: alignment changed after including header, may be due to missing #pragma pack(pop)
```

The packing goes wrong right after the out-of-class definition of `RequestFeatures<FeatureListT>::MissingFeatureNames` in `detail/RequestFeatures.hh`. A `#pragma pack(show)` after it prints 16 and then 8 (16 is the x64 default), and the next header the file opens reports C4103.

It looks like a compiler bug. It reproduces without gz headers:

```cpp
#include <set>
#include <string>

template <typename T> class Ptr {};

template <typename L>
struct R
{
  template <typename P, typename F, template <typename, typename> class E>
  static std::set<std::string> Missing(const Ptr<E<P, F>> &);
};

template <typename L>
template <typename P, typename F, template <typename, typename> class E>
std::set<std::string> R<L>::Missing(const Ptr<E<P, F>> &)
{
  std::set<std::string> names;
  return names;
}

#pragma pack(show)  // C4810: 16, then 8
#include <atomic>   // C4103
```

It needs a member template with a template template parameter, defined out of class, whose return type does not depend on the member template's parameters. `From` returns `EntityPtr<EntityT<...>>` and is clean. So are a type parameter instead of the template template parameter, the definition inside the class, `/Zc:twoPhase-` (or no `/permissive-`) and `/std:c++latest`.

## Impact

* On x64 no struct layout changed in our checks (an `alignas(16)` member keeps offset 16 under pack 8), so the warning looks cosmetic.
* MSBuild builds have the same wrong packing but do not print the warning: the compiler messages after the bug fires are lost there, while the same `cl` command run alone prints them. It shows up with the Ninja generator, where the warnings quality gate turns the gz-sim Windows jobs UNSTABLE. This blocks moving the Windows CI to Ninja (gazebo-tooling/release-tools#1600).

## Fix

Wrap the definition in `#pragma pack(push)` / `#pragma pack(pop)`, under `#ifdef _MSC_VER`. The packing after it stays 16 and the gz-sim files above compile without C4103. A `#pragma warning(disable: 4103)` in gz-physics does not help, because the warning is reported in the including file after the header ends. PR to follow, with a backport to gz-physics9.
